For auctions, buyers are inclined to spend more and thus overestimate staffing.
Neurologists and economic scientists from the University of New York have now succeeded in discovering the neurological-economic foundations of this well-known and apparent irr-rational behaviour.
According to a study published in the "Science" magazine, people don’t want to admit defeat in a competition, such as an auction on eBay, and are therefore prepared to pay even excessive prices.
The explorers attempt to explore the neurological diagram of this "game" with experimental auctions.
A group of volunteers were supposed to gamble, while the other participated in a playful action - in both occurrence - in both occupies the active brain regions of the volunteers were examined using functional magnetic resonance (fMRI).
In both games the participants were able to gain money, however an increased activity of the striatum, a complex below the cerebrospinal cortex, was measured by losers of the auction, in which surviving the opponent play an important role plays an important role.
Besides many other functions, this brain region is responsible for "independent rewards" of the nervous system.
Researchers therefore said the price increase could be explained by avoiding an overreaction of the striatum.
To underpin their thesis, the economists have additional investigated investigating the follow-up of money in the research group.
In this case, three groups were supposed against one partner under various conditions.
Accordingly, the control team was communicated the true value of the product, after which they switched.
Furthermore, an other team promised 15 bucks rewards, in the event that they emerge as winners from the game.
The third group received 15 dollars before the auction, which were also lost in the event of a lost game.
The difference between the number between the number of groups thus included the emphasis on winning, while the other lay on losing.
As proof for the hypothesis expressed by neurologists, however, the members of the "nalousing" group were prepared for higher stakes than those involved in the "winning" group.
